> **3c. V16 — WHAT EXPEDITING COSTS, WHAT IS PRESERVED, HOW THE ORDER STAYS VISIBLE.** COLLECTION HISTORY, COMPLETE (V19 audit M2): the collector CLI logs EVERY attempt — `--log` is mandatory — including refusals and pre-parse/I-O errors (`collector-collect`, `collector-verify`, `collector-refusal`, `collector-error`); the builder `build_corpus_identity_v20.py` (`f3aa4955c150798fb255d6d9d89217e0872981b09fca6d8e845d2106358d70e0`) logs every path — pre-parse refusals (`builder-pre-parse-refusal`), every verdict, witness/adoption refusals, the first ACCEPT (`builder-accept`, binding) and any conflicting later ACCEPT (`builder-conflict`, then `COLLECTION-LOCKED`) — to the append-only `_optionA_dev/collection_log.jsonl`; the identity carries the log's digest and first ACCEPT, its T_pulse, round, source and the adoption digest; the log is committed with the identity and the retained beacon record (E3 witness). ADOPTION BINDING: `_optionA_dev/ADOPTED_RULE_SHA256.txt` (exactly one 64-hex line and one newline) is committed WITH the approval record; the builder refuses unless it is a byte-equal blob at the witnessed approval commit and equals `--rule-sha256`. THE DRIVER'S CONJUNCTION (V19 audit M1): `run_configurations_v4.py` (`d55ea70ddb27ba3b849204addeec3da0551657b7a0bb3d53a422d61f9a7d7993`) refuses to load an identity unless — in addition to the seal and freeze witness — its outcome is exactly `ACCEPT-DRAND` and its source the pinned chain; its approval witness carries an entire PushEvent for the protected ref delivering the approval commit with server time before T_pulse, and a nonce; the committed collection log's bytes match the identity, are the blob at the witnessed commit, and, PARSED, contain a first `builder-accept` naming the identity's record, outcome and seed with no `builder-conflict`; the adoption file is well-formed, equals the adopted digest, is a byte-equal blob at the approval commit, and matches the identity's adoption digest; the retained beacon record matches the identity's digest and the seed RE-DERIVED from it by the drand-only verdict equals the identity's seed. Fixtures: `test_build_corpus_identity_v20.py` (`3eb80459d9ba9e8226a2367e1d2a7426e12895bc9f9cad8a28b48de9e0172c07`, 6 tests on the real round: accepted with history; every attempt logged incl. RETRY, witness refusal and pre-parse; a second collection locked out; adoption must be a blob at the approval commit; identity fields); `test_run_configurations_v4.py` (`512384b0112aec56a4113db9d42dd0f9acc3c0df0d95eb1ddc3c53ab9502e196`, 20 tests; the acceptance conjunction refuses each of eleven defective identities — RETRY, invented and NIST outcomes, wrong source, missing/late witness, lock mismatch, log digest, logged conflict, malformed adoption, non-rederivable seed — and an emptied log after the seal; its end-to-end test drives the real round through the V20 builder into the driver; run under the pinned interpreter with the venv's packages on PYTHONPATH — E2's interpreter lock is unchanged). COST: (i) the NIST primary is, in practice, given up — under V15 the fallback could not open before T_pulse + 24 h, so NIST had at least that long to serve a verifying certificate and was checked live at each later build; under V17 NIST is tried live at build time, at or after T_pulse, once, so the seed will almost certainly come from drand, whose BLS proof this lane cannot verify (LIMIT (ii)); the seed's provenance therefore rests on agreement of ≥ 2 of 4 pinned relays, retained and live, not on a signature verified to a pinned root — a weaker provenance, accepted with eyes open; (ii) the V15 signature and its RETRY record become history: one signed commitment whose seed was never drawn, on file forever, and the register will show a V15 signed-then-superseded row; (iii) nothing else — no sample size, floor, bar, exclusion, attempt count, custody or protection rule moves. PRESERVED: V15's bytes and digest, its signature record, the RETRY beacon record, every pin V15 carries except the three files named above. ORDER: the server-side push event (W3) and the nonce (W5) of step (1a) bound the approval record on both sides; MIN_T_SIGN and EXCLUDED_T_PULSE are lower bounds only; the change record carries the timeline (finding 11:01, dispatch 11:06, confirmation 11:08, code 11:12–11:14, this text 11:16, gate, approval, T_pulse) so a reader sees request → authorisation → approval → pulse in that order.
